  "ValidationReference": "The official land-use maps of Florida's five water management districts, photo-interpreted from aerial imagery of 2019--2023 in the FLUCCS classification \\citep{fdot1999,fgdl_fluccs}, are an independent reference for both the fields and the pasture they miss. Pasture in them (FLUCCS 2110--2130, and 2100 cropland and pastureland in the Southwest Florida district, which does not separate the two) covers 80\\,\\% of the area of tier~A fields and 69\\,\\% of tier~B fields (78\\,\\% together); a further 6\\,\\% is mapped as crops (field crops, which include hay, row crops, groves and nurseries), 3\\,\\% as horse farms, dairies or feeding operations, 3\\,\\% as rangeland or rural open land, 3\\,\\% as wetland (mostly wet prairie and marsh), 2\\,\\% as mined or reclaimed land, 2\\,\\% as low-density or rural residential land, 2\\,\\% as other urban or transportation land and 1\\,\\% as forest. 72\\,\\% of fields are mostly pasture in the reference. Of the 1\\,516 thousand hectares of reference pasture, 45\\,\\% lies inside tier~A and B fields and 7\\,\\% inside tier~C fields; 14\\,\\% was never mapped as pasture by the CDL, 19\\,\\% in fewer than 6 of the ten years, 3\\,\\% was removed by the woody screen, 6\\,\\% lies outside parcels or in parts smaller than 2\\,ha and 7\\,\\% in fields left out by the selection. The shares above exclude tier~C, whose fields were taken from this reference and are therefore 100\\,\\% pasture in it by construction.",
  "ValidationFields": "ESA WorldCover 2021 \\citep{zanaga2022}, which is derived from Sentinel-1 and Sentinel-2 independently of the CDL, maps 89\\,\\% of the area of tier~A fields and 89\\,\\% of tier~B fields as grassland and a further 3\\,\\% and 4\\,\\% as cropland, which in Florida includes hay and improved pasture (92.4\\,\\% of the area of all fields together). Trees cover 7\\,\\% (A) and 6\\,\\% (B) and herbaceous wetland 0\\,\\% and 0\\,\\%. Built-up land is below 0.2\\,\\% in both tiers. This is agreement on land cover, not a measure of grazing use or of boundary accuracy. Tier~C fields were selected as open in WorldCover, which maps 98\\,\\% of their area as grassland or cropland and 2\\,\\% as trees; this is by construction.",
  "ValidationCoverage": "Of all field-level HLS records, 88\\,\\% passed the clear-pixel test; the geometric shift was estimated for 97\\,\\% of the records, 71\\,\\% over the clear field pixels of the granule and 27\\,\\% over a clear 512 by 512 block of the tile where that window was smaller than 64 by 64 pixels, and exceeded one pixel in 0.03\\,\\%; the 2.6\\,\\% not assessed (1.8\\,\\% of the used records) are kept and marked (\\texttt{qa\\_geometry\\_status}). Of the clear records, 0.02\\,\\% and 0.01\\,\\% were rejected for reflectance outside [$-0.01$, 1] or blue reflectance above 0.25; 88\\,\\% of all records were used. Counting one observation per field and acquisition day, the median field has 36 usable optical observations per year (median over 2016--2025; yearly medians 17--58, increasing as Landsat~9 and Sentinel-2C joined) and 26 Sentinel-1 acquisition days. Of all field $\\times$ 5-day cells, 45\\,\\% hold an observed optical composite (50\\,\\% in October--May, 35\\,\\% in June--September), 43\\,\\% an interpolated one (gaps of at most 30 days) and 12\\,\\% none; interpolated values are therefore 48\\,\\% of the NDVI values in the cube.",
  "ValidationPhysical": "Statewide median NDVI reaches its minimum in February and its maximum in August. At the end of the dry season (April--May) the anomaly of observed NDVI is correlated with the 60-day rainfall anomaly ($r=0.26$, $n=3\\,413\\,213$ field-periods, not independent). Sentinel-1 VV backscatter is higher in periods with at least 25\\,mm of rain than in periods with at most 1\\,mm for 98\\,\\% of fields (median difference 0.8\\,dB). Hurricane Ian brought at least 200\\,mm in 26 Sep--05 Oct 2022 to 12\\,802 fields. Among them, the change of observed MNDWI after landfall was -0.03 (IQR -0.05 to -0.00; 95\\,\\% interval of the median -0.04 to -0.02; $n=3\\,382$) for fields with HAND of 1\\,m or less, -0.03 (IQR -0.04 to -0.01; 95\\,\\% interval of the median -0.03 to -0.02; $n=4\\,584$) for 1--3\\,m and -0.02 (IQR -0.03 to -0.01; 95\\,\\% interval of the median -0.02 to -0.02; $n=3\\,193$) for 3\\,m or more; the change of VV was -0.4 (IQR -1.0 to 0.1; 95\\,\\% interval of the median -0.6 to -0.3; $n=3\\,816$), -0.5 (IQR -0.9 to -0.0; 95\\,\\% interval of the median -0.6 to -0.3; $n=5\\,296$) and -0.4 (IQR -0.9 to 0.0; 95\\,\\% interval of the median -0.5 to -0.3; $n=3\\,688$)\\,dB (Fig.~\\ref{fig:events}).",
  "ValidationEvents": "In the 5-day period starting 15 Jan 2018, 34\\,491 fields had a mean minimum temperature of 2\\,$^\\circ$C or lower and only 8 one of 8\\,$^\\circ$C or higher, too few for a comparison of cold and mild fields. Over the following 15 days observed NDVI changed by -0.08 (IQR -0.11 to -0.05; 95\\,\\% interval of the median -0.09 to -0.07; $n=31\\,118$) in the cold fields, and VV by 0.4 (IQR 0.1 to 0.8; 95\\,\\% interval of the median 0.4 to 0.5; $n=27\\,391$)\\,dB. In the same cold fields and calendar periods of the other years without a freeze, NDVI changed by a median of -0.03, so the 2018 change minus the usual one was -0.05 (IQR -0.08 to -0.02; 95\\,\\% interval of the median -0.06 to -0.04; $n=30\\,326$; 91\\,\\% of fields below their usual change). Over 525 weeks the statewide median anomaly of observed NDVI is correlated with the weekly NASS pasture condition index ($r=0.35$) and with the index after removing its mean for the week of year ($r=0.54$; Spearman 0.52); weeks are serially correlated and the yearly values range from 0.03 to 0.84.",
  "ValidationIanPasses": "On single Sentinel-1 passes of the same orbit, 4 days after Ian's landfall (20 Sep against 2 Oct 2022), VV dropped by more than 3\\,dB in 5.6\\,\\% [3.8\\,\\%, 7.9\\,\\%] of 3\\,777 fields, against 1.8\\,\\% between the two passes before the storm (difference 3.8\\,\\% [1.5\\,\\%, 5.9\\,\\%]) among fields with at least 200\\,mm and HAND of 1\\,m or less; in 1.5\\,\\% [0.6\\,\\%, 2.4\\,\\%] of 5\\,270 fields, against 0.6\\,\\% between the two passes before the storm (difference 0.9\\,\\% [0.1\\,\\%, 1.7\\,\\%]) with HAND of 1--3\\,m; in 1.0\\,\\% [0.5\\,\\%, 1.5\\,\\%] of 3\\,682 fields, against 0.4\\,\\% between the two passes before the storm (difference 0.6\\,\\% [-0.1\\,\\%, 1.1\\,\\%]) with HAND of 3\\,m or more; and in 0.5\\,\\% [0.4\\,\\%, 0.7\\,\\%] of 13\\,222 fields, against 2.0\\,\\% between the two passes before the storm (difference -1.4\\,\\% [-2.4\\,\\%, -0.3\\,\\%]) among fields with less than 50\\,mm.",
  "ValidationQuality": "12 fields have no optical or radar observation at all and 75 more have radar only; they are narrow fields in which no pixel remains after the inward buffers, kept in the release and flagged (\\texttt{flag\\_no\\_observations}, \\texttt{flag\\_no\\_optical}). 1.5\\,\\% of the radar composites combine acquisitions from more than one relative orbit (0.9\\,\\% mix ascending and descending passes), and the set of relative orbits differs from the field's previous observed period in 47\\,\\% of the composites, so the incidence angle varies within and between periods; the per-acquisition table keeps the orbit of every value. Because indices are computed per pixel and limited to $[-1, 1]$, NDVI composites reach $\\pm 1$ in 0.002\\,\\% of the observed cells. Every field is a single 4-connected part. PRISM daily values become stable about six months after the date; the record was retrieved on 24 November 2026, so values after 24 May 2026 are provisional and may be revised in later PRISM releases.",
  "ValidationSummary": "that 92\\,\\% of the field area (tier~A 92\\,\\%, tier~B 93\\,\\%) is grassland or cropland in the independent ESA WorldCover map and that the median field has 36 usable optical observations per year"
}